Satellite Communication: State-of-the-Art and
Future Challenges
Muhammad Nauman Danish, Syed Ahmed Pasha,
Abstract—Beyond line of sight communication is important as it provides, for example, tactical data sharing for military advantage. Satellite communication (SATCOM) is the only technology that provides this capability. Recent advances in micro-electronics technology have significantly enhanced the capability of SATCOM systems in terms of their ability to reprogram and reconfigure. Moreover, signal processing units can be simulated using software-defined radios. But a growing demand for high data rates and user anonymity, requiring implementation of higher-order modulation, multiplexing and efficient spread spectrum schemes, pose new challenges for SATCOM. These are compounded in the presence of reliability and low latency requirements for tactical communication as well as communication between various satellite technologies in the future such as constellations and 5G technology. In this survey, we review the state-of-the-art in SATCOM systems with respect to modulation, multiplexing, channel encoding and software-based implementations and discuss future challenges.
Index Terms—Satellite communication, high-throughput, software-defined radio, modulation, multiplexing, channel encoding
I. INTRODUCTION
S
PACE technology has a huge impact on the economic and social aspects of both commercial as well as military domains. Driven by remarkable advances in the microelectronics technology in recent years, building of smaller satellites by smaller teams has become a reality resulting in rapid development of multi-orbit satellite systems. According to an estimate1, 2,666 operational satellites were circling the globe in April 2020 with > 70% in the low earth orbit. More than a third were communication satellites, > 15% were earth observing satellites and about 4% were navigation and positioning satellites. These have the potential to hugely impact a number of areas such as navigation, communication, weather prediction and disaster management while facilitating beyond line of sight communication in multiple orbits. Recent developments in satellite technology have opened the landscape for space communication with reprogrammable and reconfigurable capability offering low cost, fast, more frequent, and broader access to space. Satellite technology has vast military applications enabling network-centric battle force capability along with tactical data sharing between multigeneration aircraft.
