The rapid advancements in satellite technology have exploited multiple orbits for satellite communication (SATCOM) systems. To minimize aerodynamic drag, satellites are launched into space to orbit the earth at altitudes well above 300 km. In the geosynchronous orbit (GSO), a geosynchronous equatorial orbit (GEO) satellite is launched above the equator and orbits the earth at an altitude of 35,786 km with an orbital velocity matching that of the earth so that it appears at a fixed position in the sky. Thus, it can be used as a reliable communication relay as well as for providing broadcast services over a large geographical area. The non-geostationary-satellite orbit (NGSO) comprises multiple orbits at various altitudes, namely, low earth orbit (LEO) (< 2, 000 km), medium earth orbit (MEO) (2,000-35,786 km), polar orbit (200-1,000 km) and highly elliptical orbit (HEO). Non-GEO satellites are available over a certain area on the earth for a brief duration only and therefore a group or constellation of satellites is needed for uninterrupted connectivity. Moreover, the ground segment requires a satellite tracking mechanism to communicate with non-GEO satellites if directional antennas are used. The coverage of GSO and NGSO satellites is illustrated in Fig. 12 and a comparison is summarized in Table I. Table II is a list of frequently occurring acronyms throughout the paper. The basic architecture of a communication satellite involves a ground terminal, a space terminal, and equipment for communication protocols comprising a SATCOM modem, block up-convertor (BUC), intermediate frequency (IF) cables, lownoise block (LNB), dish antennas and a centralized facility monitoring and controlling the communication network. Fig. 2 illustrates the basic architecture of a SATCOM system. The space terminal provides communication routing for the ground terminals. It has the capability to manage access control adaptively and in a multi-beam setup, implements the spot-beam management system [1]. The earth-based equipment (ground terminal) incorporates gateways with fiber-optic connectivity to provide end-user services. The (centralized) monitoring facility comprises multiple network control and management centers (HUB) that can provide real-time data services [2].
