The HUB facility configures the network, allocates available resources, maintains network architecture and ensures user anonymity. The terminals in a SATCOM system comprise both fixed and mobile user ends that can access the SATCOM network directly or through terrestrial access points. The SATCOM modem, a central component of the ground terminal, performs, for example, data conversions, modulation, channel encoding, multiplexing and communication protocols while converting base band to radio frequency (RF) band and vice versa. The processing units for SATCOM based on signal processing devices can be realized using a software-defined radio (SDR). Table III summarizes the characteristics of some commercially available SATCOM modems. Technological developments in electronics have made it possible for satellite manufacturers and operators to consider digital payloads with on-board processing capability. Thus, limitations in ground processing for SATCOM systems are overcome by modern signal processing techniques. For example, delay in processing systems is mitigated by reducing processing and acquisition delays. Similarly, efficient resource utilization is enabled by scheduling capability and flexibility to integrate with new hardware [3]. These techniques have been used for on-board pre-distortion reduction and energy detection in [4], [5]. The weight and size of a satellite are important considerations for launching the satellite into space. Light-weight and small size satellites with advanced signal processing hardware is the modern paradigm leading to small satellite constellations and high throughput satellites (HTS) offering broadband internet-based applications with rapid media streaming for under-served areas. Moreover, seamless communication between 5G communication systems and small satellite constellations offering uninterrupted communication
services has the potential to integrate advanced wired and wireless systems and protocols for particular requirements and desired applications. This will usher in a new space era facilitating protocol development for mega-constellations, machineto-machine communication (M2M), ultra-reliable low-latency communication (URLLC), and HTS systems. The next generation of satellites demands an evolutionary design to address current challenges such as internet access to far-end users, geographical limitations of GEO, MEO and LEO satellites [6], advanced on-board processing capability, efficient tracking antennas to complement LEO satellite operations, robust inter-satellite links [7], real-time applications based on lower orbit satellite constellation operations to exploit smaller propagation delay [8], advanced routing and addressing protocols to ensure quality of service [1], software and hardware upgrades based on new communication and networking protocols to cope with new user requirements [9], [10], interoperability between multi-orbit satellites and operators [11], and seamless integration of existing satellite architecture and terrestrial network [12]. These challenges can potentially be overcome by operational capabilities of software-defined networking (SDN) [13], network virtualization (NV) [14], network function virtualization (NFV) [15] and SDR [16]. The existing body of work on advanced SATCOM systems [3], [17]–[33] is concerned with specific technologies for 5G and 6G networks and the role of satellites in these networks. In this survey paper, we aim to bridge the gap between developments in SDR-based ground stations, corresponding technologies and advancements in communication protocols with respect to multiplexing, modulation, and channel encoding in a structured way and highlight the main challenges, solutions and open problems. Moreover, we identify new research directions in advanced wireless communication technologies for future SATCOM systems. More specifically:
