II. MULTIPLE ACCESS CHANNEL MODELS FOR SATCOM
One of the primary functions performed by a SATCOM modem is multiplexing. For high throughput, bandwidth sharing approaches based on frequency division multiple access (FDMA), time division multiple access (TDMA), code division multiple access (CDMA), spatial division multiple access (SDMA), orthogonal multiple access (OMA) and nonorthogonal multiple access (NOMA) are used. FDMA is the simplest to implement but requires allocation of distinct frequency bands to each subscriber. TDMA is effective but requires framing and synchronization which is problematic in environments with significant propagation delay. CDMA shares the available band, based on pseudorandom patterns for multi-user environments, by assigning unique random patterns to each subscriber. This allocation enables the use of multiple codes on the same frequency channel for different users. At the receiver, segregation of the codes can be achieved using a correlator and filter to despread the signal for the intended user. Typically, CDMA is coupled with a spread spectrum technique, e.g., direct sequence (DS)-CDMA and frequency hopping (FH)-CDMA [34]–[40]. DS spreading entails multiplication of information bits with a pseudorandom pattern. In FH spreading, the bandwidth is distributed into multiple sub-frequency bands. Then, information is transmitted based on a certain hopping pattern. The hopping pattern results in different channels and interfering signals for the intended transmission in each hop, avoiding the unintended signal. FH-CDMA consumes less power and requires low budget equipment while DS-CDMA has shown greater reliability and efficiency. Moreover, it has outperformed modern multiplexing


