A. Primary Multiple Access Schemes
For modems using FDMA, TDMA, and CDMA for SATCOM, interference problems are mitigated if separate frequency bands for the uplink and downlink are used [42]. As already mentioned, FDMA is limited by the requirement of separate frequency bands, TDMA requires strict synchronization protocols, i.e., frame synchronization, carrier recovery, symbol recovery, loop-back block, orbital effects catering block and other synchronizing blocks, while CDMA is limited by bandwidth expansion factors and low data rates. Spread spectrum multiple access (SSMA) with binary phase shift keying (BPSK) and orthogonal convolutional codes (CC) has shown that signal spreading using forward error correction (FEC) coding with code rates ≤ 1 fully exploits the processing gain and achieves the ultimate Shannon capacity for multiple access channel models [43]. However, this performance is only achieved for very low rate FEC codes having large bandwidth
expansion of multiples of 2K with pseudorandom sequences where K is the sequence constraint length. For dedicated channels and a reasonable signal-to-noise ratio (SNR), SSMA has a lower bandwidth efficiency than FDMA but unlike FDMA, is not limited by the requirement of dedicated channels. Due to the random access nature of CDMA coupled with reasonable degradation and appropriate ranging methods, BPSK-DSSS has been proposed for ground-satellite mobile stations with L-band frequency and 3 MHz channel bandwidth [44]. Fades of 3-7 dB due to the presence of trees and deep fades of 15 dB due to tall buildings and tunnels have been reported.
DSSS vs. FDMA: DSSS has exhibited remarkable performance to combat fading due to multipath propagation effects in cellular communication systems [36]. A study of the DSSSCDMA system with frequency selective fading channels, more specifically, 4-state Rician and 4-state shadowed distributions for intermediate circular orbit satellites has shown that quadrature phase shift keying (QPSK) with the raised cosine filter and an adaptive receiver based on the least mean square (LMS) algorithm is insensitive to timing and phase synchronization [34]. Soft decision decoding leads to an improvement in the performance under fading conditions. Moreover, an adaptive


