For very long and power limited high frequency (3-30 MHz) links, the performance of DSSS and OFDM has been studied in terms of the error and differential error vector magnitude [45], [46]. Spectral efficiency of DSSS is inversely proportional to the length of the pseudonoise (PN) sequence. For OFDM, a soft limiter is applied to check the peak-toaverage power ratio (PAPR) to avoid operating in the nonlinear
region of the amplifier. Experimental studies have shown that the longest symbol for DSSS does not guarantee the lowest bit error rate (BER) because the long symbol time exceeds the channel coherence time resulting in high phase rotation. Small symbols lead to large variations in the channel impulse response. Thus, an intermediate symbol length gives the best results. For OFDM, channel encoding and interleaving are required to reduce the BER. The data rate for OFDM is 10-15× higher compared to DSSS, resulting in 20-40× higher spectral efficiency. But in poor SNR conditions with the data rate below the actual channel capacity, DSSS can transmit data without channel encoding and interleaving. Moreover, DSSS favours longer period transmissions below the noise floor, reducing interference from other users. DSSS with multi code spread spectrum (MCSS) for wireless video transmission has been studied in [41]. The system was realized using an SDR platform and shown to outperform OFDM in terms of anti-jamming capability. However, this is accomplished at the cost of bandwidth expansion for data transmission. With a lower PAPR, DSSS yields higher power efficiency compared to OFDM with MCSS. The findings in [41] can be summarized as:
(i) DSSS outperforms OFDM with MCSS in terms of antijamming capability but OFDM has a higher spectral efficiency. (ii) MCSS performs moderately from both aspects, i.e., shows lower spectral efficiency than OFDM and lower bandwidth expansion than DSSS. MCSS shows better anti-jamming performance than OFDM but not DSSS.
