For example, for bursty traffic, a fixed assignment results in inefficient use of spectrum resources. The demand assignment multiple access (DAMA) underperforms in sporadic IoT traffic having very short packet length and low duty-cycles [48], [49]. In such environments, random access protocols are preferred which transmit data packets irrespective of the nodal and channel conditions but can lead to packet collisions. Fixed assignment protocols allocate fixed resources to each user in the network with respect to time and frequency based on the user requirements. Thus, even in simultaneous transmission from multiple users data packet collisions are avoided. Examples include OFDMA and single-carrier (SC)FDMA. However, strict synchronization in time and frequency is required to avoid co-channel interference. In narrowband (NB)-IoT, OFDM and SC-FDMA are used for the downlink and uplink communication respectively [50]. Similarly, a chirp spread spectrum signal is employed in LoRa [51] and an ultranarrowband signal with differential BPSK in SigFox. The traffic in a satellite system is controlled by the network control center (HUB) which monitors and distributes resources to meet the demands from the return channel satellite terminals (RCSTs). The use of hybrid schemes, e.g., multi-frequency (MF)-TDMA allows RCSTs to control digitally modulated carriers by applying frequency hopping and transmit short bursts of data during the assigned time slots for different users. The alternative is to use a continuous carrier at the cost of increased complexity of the RCSTs. CC is more dynamic in terms of adapting to varying transmission requirements as compared to MF-TDMA. Multi-carrier modulation schemes such as OFDM and SC-FDMA or MF-TDMA are implemented in modern day satellite routers. In broadband satellite systems where the digital video broadcast standard is used, MF-TDMA has shown suboptimal performance [48]. For NB-IoT, the time-frequency ALOHA (TFA) has been proposed in [52]. But for IoT-based satellite applications, the significant propagation delay (250-270 ms) makes ALOHA unattractive despite performing well at very modest traffic requirements [53]. For satellite-IoT systems, advanced random access schemes have been discussed in [54]. The enhanced spread spectrum ALOHA (E-SSA) [55], asynchronous contention resolution diversity ALOHA (ACRDA) [56], and con
tention resolution diversity ALOHA (CRDSA) [57] yield better spectral and energy efficiency due to the iterative successive interference cancellation (SIC) capability which improves the probability of detection of the received packets.
C. Advanced Multiple Access Schemes
Orthogonal Frequency Division Multiple Access: OFDM is the preferred scheme in multiple communication standards, e.g., DVB, DSL, WLAN, WMAN and 4G wireless networks. It consists of N adjacent orthogonal sub-carriers. The spacing between the sub-carriers depends on the Doppler effect, phase noise, radio-channel frequency selectivity and rate of channel variations. The sub-carrier spacing results in efficient demodulation at the receiver side due to reduced inter-carrier interference (ICI). The inverse fast Fourier transform (FFT)based algorithm reduces implementation complexity at the transmitter side, and assists frequency domain equalization at the receiver. OFDMA is used in 5G New Radio communication systems, i.e., mobile broadband systems with the addition of cyclic prefix (CP) [58], similar to 4G LTE systems [24]. The implementation parameters for OFDMA are sub-carrier spacing, number of sub-carriers, and CP length [59]. SATCOM suffers from multipath fading. A potential approach to mitigate this phenomenon is the space frequency block code (SFBC)-OFDM. Somasekhar et al. [60] has discussed an algorithm to reduce ICI based on time selectivity and inter-symbol interference (ISI) induced by frequency selectivity and furthermore discussed the energy efficient resource utilization and numerical efficacy of the system. In [61], an SDR-based so-called network coded cooperation (NCC)OFDMA has been demonstrated using three nodes capable of transmitting and receiving data. The combination of NCC and OFDMA has been shown to improve robustness and reliability of the wireless communication system against channel fading impediments. The performance has been evaluated using BER and error vector magnitude. Filtered OFDMA (F-OFDMA), in addition to CP, involves spectrum shaping in each sub-band to reduce out-of-band (OOB) transmissions and uses guard intervals to suppress inter-sub-band interferences. However, it suffers from loss in time domain orthogonality, inner-sub-band interferences, and increased complexity [62]–[64]. Generalized frequency division multiplexing (GFDM), in addition to N sub-carriers, assigns different time slots for Ns sub-symbols, resulting in additional degree of freedom with Ns N modulated constellation symbols [65], [66]. GFDM has the advantage that it requires only one CP for the entire transmission block resulting in higher spectral efficiency, and has lower OOB emissions than OFDM. However, it is prone to ISI and ICI, high PAPR and high implementation complexity [18], [67]. Multiple equalization and interference cancellation techniques have been proposed, e.g., matched filtering, zero forcing, double-sided SIC, SIC [66]–[72] to reduce the interferences between sub-carriers and time slots. Multiple architectures have been proposed to reduce the complexity for future wireless communication systems [67], [73]. Time-interleaved block-windowed burst OFDM (TIBWB-OFDM) implements


