power spectral density based spectral confinement and windowing techniques to lower OOB emissions resulting in higher spectral efficiency than cyclic prefix-OFDMA (CP-OFDMA). Moreover, instead of CP, it employs concatenation of symbols with addition of single prefix of zeros. Thus, requiring lower power for transmission resulting in increased power efficiency [74]–[76]. However, the improvement in spectral efficiency is limited by the size of the OFDM windowed-based blocks. As the average transmitted signal power is reduced due to windowing operations, the overall PAPR grows [77]. In [78], the intentional interference mitigation capability for wireless systems has been studied and a secure pre-coded (SP)OFDM system has been proposed for environments where a jammer can cause communication failure. The synchronization performance in additive white Gaussian noise (AWGN) and multipath fading channels has shown that SP-OFDM is superior to traditional OFDM and achieves positive capacity compared to zero under disguised jamming. For traditional OFDM systems in AWGN and Rician channels, the BER cannot be reduced by decreasing the code rate if proper antijamming procedure is not adopted. But in SP-OFDM, the BER can be significantly reduced with a decrease in the code rate. Both traditional OFDM and SP-OFDM show high spectral efficiency. Besides the choice between fixed assignment and random access protocols, efficient use of the available spectrum can also be achieved using the dynamic spectrum sharing (DSS) technique which enables spectral coexistence of multiple wireless communication systems in the available radio spectrum. The DSS approach implements coordinated spectrum sharing, e.g., TV whitespace database or coordinated spectrum sensing. A study on DSS models [79], has discussed three types: the common model, the shared-use model and the exclusive use model. In the common model, all users have equal rights in terms of spectrum access. In the exclusive use model, secondary users have dedicated privilege to use the spectrum and in the shared-use model, users utilize the vacant and underutilized spectrum in interference avoidance or opportunistic manner [80]. In heterogeneous networks, spectrum sharing is based on advanced mechanisms, e.g., licensed assisted access (LAA), channel bonding (CB), licensed shared access (LSA), carrier aggregation (CA) and spectrum access system (SAS) [17], [81].
Non-Orthogonal Multiple Access: Non-orthogonal multiple access (NOMA) is a promising technique for channel access in 5G systems [82]. Unlike OMA, it allows multiple users to share the same time-frequency resources. The cochannel interference resulting from reuse of the spectrum by multiple terminals can be mitigated using SIC and a multiuser detection system at the receiver station. Thus, increasing spectrum utilization with respect to the available number of users in 5G terrestrial scenarios [82]–[85]. The performance of NOMA in the 5G terrestrial-satellite network with respect to resource optimization, beamforming and power utilization has been studied in [86]. In [87], the power coefficients and beamforming weight vectors have been obtained to achieve a desired performance. In [88], NOMA has been proposed for the Integrated
Satellite-Terrestrial Communication Networks (ISTCN) in 6G networks to overcome congestion and interference issues due to the use of common bands. 6G networks aim to provide a high speed and persistent network using efficient spectrum management and dynamic spectrum sharing with the help of NOMA and cognitive radios. The proposed network and network access model comprise HEO and LEO satellites with terrestrial cellular stems based on a hybrid network access model. For smooth spectrum sharing between the space and ground elements, user terminals are grouped at the satellite gateway based on the received CSI for uplink communication. For downlink, SIC is used for the receiver terminals. Opportunistic spectrum access is promoted for satellite users to suppress possible interference, i.e., based on available information, if a given channel is not in use by the terrestrial system, a satellite user may occupy it; otherwise, the user needs to switch to an idle channel. This is achieved using an interference cognition technique that can identify the interferences and provide anti-interference decision. Furthermore, power control using cognitive radios and satellite beamforming can reduce potential interference and congestion of spectrum in ISTCN. Proactive content caching in hybrid satellite aerial networks (HSATN) can reduce transmission delay, improve spectral utility, and support wider connectivity for next generation wireless networks [89]. The system model for HSATN comprises the terrestrial systems, aerial platforms and satellite spacecrafts. The request for content from multiple users can be accommodated by the cache enabled aerial relays. The content caching model is based on content popularity skewness while the aerial platforms maintain finite content catalogue. However, if the requested file is not cached, the satellite system will directly accommodate the request using NOMA. During the content delivery phase, NOMA allows satellites to push the required content to the aerial relay. For uplink communication, NOMA can reduce the frequency interference and coordination problem in multi-layer satellite networks (MLSN) [90]. MLSN can exploit the high throughput capacity of GEO satellites and the short delay in LEO constellations to meet 5G network requirements. A two layer system model for uplink communication has been proposed employing LEO and GEO satellites, with better channel condition than for LEO satellites alone. The N NOMA groups based on the user pairing scheme comprise a single GEO and multiple LEO users. The pairing is based on a maxmin strategy to maximize the minimum channel difference between user pairs. The path difference between the GEO and LEO satellites creates an effective power attenuation. Thus, the GEO satellite can decode the GEO users’ information without SIC. However, for LEO satellites SIC is used to decode the intended information. Multi-beam capability offers users to reuse the available spectrum in a much more efficient way by exploiting the spatial characteristics of transmission and/or reception of antenna arrays. For example, the active electronically scanned array antenna delivers a cutting edge high throughput SATCOM system based on SDMA. But it requires advanced digital signal processing techniques to mitigate multi-user interference to achieve the desired performance. Such SATCOM systems


