lead to multi-user, multiple-input and multiple-output (MIMO) systems (also commonly known as multi-beam joint processing systems) [3]. The linear beamforming (also known as precoding) method effectively avoids multi-user interference [91]–[94]. Extension of beamforming techniques for managing interference at SATCOM gateway systems is a recent development in broadband multi-beam SATCOM systems [95]. Beamforming for designing multiple data streams within the same bandwidth, takes advantage of channel state information (CSI) to mitigate multi-user interference for user terminals resulting in high signal-to-interference-plus-noise ratio (SINR) for specific users. An alternative to precoding multiple data streams is symbol level precoding based on CSI and data information of transmitted symbols [96]. The idea is to control the interference effects to produce constructive results at each user terminal resulting in reduced power consumption for a given SINR as compared to traditional techniques. To meet the internet broadband services demand for nonuniform and distributed users, i.e., aeronautical, fixed satellite services users and maritime users, the adaptive multibeam high throughput satellite system has been proposed [33] based on the four colour frequency reuse scheme [97]. The neighbouring cells have different carrier frequency or polarization and the space segment can alter the beam size and position using a programmable payload. The beam patterns are elliptical which add more freedom for optimization and are approximated by a two-dimensional Gaussian function. The broadband users are clustered for geographical grouping based on the weighted k-means clustering algorithm. For coverage and beam borders identification, the Voronoi tessellation is used with irregular polygons approximated by ellipses. The proposed method distributes the traffic across all beams equally resulting in better frequency and payload allocation, and radio resource management. Angular domain design parameters have been used for satellite beam pattern and footprint parameters. To summarize, FDMA suffers from interference and requires separate bands for each user which reduces spectral efficiency. It does not scale with the number users due to the requirement of guard bands and adjacent channel interference. While TDMA is effective, it requires frame and orbital effects synchronization, carrier and symbol clock recovery, and loopback synchronization which make it difficult to implement in the presence of significant propagation delay and diversity of user terminal. SDMA offers better spectral efficiency and synchronization complexity but requires the reuse distance in the network architecture to be preserved. CDMA can accommodate multiple users within the same bandwidth without the need for synchronization, is well-integrated with cellular technologies, and does not suffer from interference due to separate code word allocation. But these benefits come at the cost of higher complexity, and the need for guard band and guard time. Moreover, the overall quality of services can deteriorate if the number of users is increased. The performance of the variants of CDMA, namely DSSS and FHSS can vary. In the multipath and intentional interference environment, DSSS outperforms FHSS in the presence of noise but is limited by high acquisition time, wideband characteristics, and
the requirement of sequence generation at high rates. DSSS with an adaptive receiver instead of the conventional matched filtering enhances capacity of the system. A low rate spread spectrum technique for poor channel forecast can increase the integrity of the received data, whereas the multi-carrier OFDM technique for sporadic burst of data at higher rates when the channel forecast is good increases the transmission data rate. MCSS performance shows a trade-off between reliability and data rate. In comparison to OFDM, DSSS and MCSS effectively resist narrowband interference at the cost of bandwidth expansion. The advantages and limitations of some multiple access schemes for SATCOM systems achieving interference mitigation and reduced complexity are summarized in Table V. A suitable methodology can be selected based on the intended application of the SATCOM system. For SATCOM systems, utilization of the available spectrum depends on the type of traffic used for data transmission. Both fixed and random access protocols can be used for services each with its advantages and limitations. For sporadic and modest transmissions random access protocols, e.g., ALOHA, E-SSA, ACRDA and CRDSA can provide better efficiency at the expense of packet collisions. Fixed assignment protocols, e.g., MF-TDMA and SC-FDMA are suitable for continuous and large packet transmission, avoiding data packet collisions at the expense of spectrum efficiency. Dynamic spectrum sharing capability is recommended to use the under-utilized and vacant spectrum. Similarly, multi-beam capability can enhance capacity of the SATCOM system in terms of high throughput and spectrum sharing used in, e.g., OMA and NOMA. NOMA has been studied for satellite-terrestrial integrated networks. However, both NOMA and OMA need to mitigate co-channel interference for successful sharing of the available spectrum. The use of NOMA along with SDR can create the opportunity for dynamic spectrum sharing that can further enhance utilization of the available spectrum.
III. SATCOM CHANNEL MODELLING AND MODULATION
SCHEMES
Satellites in the geostationary orbit are ideal for SATCOM. A single geostationary satellite (GeoSAT) has greater coverage than medium and low earth satellites. Multi-beam capability enables the satellite system to reuse the frequency which results in higher throughput as compared to terrestrial networks. But the delay in GeoSATs precludes telephonic services for users. Constellations in the LEO orbit with cutting edge communication technology and modest launching overhead make LEO satellites a viable option for low latency and consistent coverage for SATCOM services. This is evident from the growing number of companies such as Amazon, TeleSAT, SpaceX, and OneWeb taking this approach [30]. SpaceX, had contributed 242 satellites to the Starlink constellations by January 2020. Similarly, O3B has launched a constellation comprising 20 MEO satellites above the equator with steerable antennas. Combination of satellites in different orbits results in the formation of hybrid systems with more advanced communication capabilities and greater coverage. For example, the


