A. GSO and NGSO SATCOM Channel Modelling
For SATCOM, the communication channel is crucial due to the considerable distance between the transmitting and receiving terminals. The characteristics of the medium are not uniform between the terminals and depend on the frequency range used for the communication system. For fixed satellite systems, the channel is typically modelled for C-band (4-6 GHz). But for modern and future satellite systems, higher bands such as the Ku-band and Ka-band are preferred. The propagation model and attenuation characteristics for these three bands are not the same resulting in different channel models with different data rate, range, and reliability of transmission. Consequently, to study the channel characteristics, multiple models including the AWGN, hybrid fading, lognormal fading, Rician and Rayleigh fading models need to be considered [34], [43], [44], [98]–[102]. The fixed satellite channel can be modelled as AWGN [98] with first-order statistics modelling various atmospheric fading phenomena such as tropospheric scintillation, gaseous absorption and clouds, attenuation due to precipitation and signal depolarization. For SATCOM systems, the link margin is critical as it ensures the availability of a link in poor weather conditions. The effect of rain on the SATCOM link can be modelled using dynamic channel effects that characterize the temporal properties of the AWGN channel. Second-order statistics describe fade slope and duration. The relative distance between the ground terminals and the satellite compared to the onboard antenna distances leads to correlation in the fading and thus adversely affects MIMO systems [30].
For fixed SATCOM systems, the distance between the ground terminals needs to be significant to ensure de-correlation of fading in the rain [98], [103].
Modelling the satellite channel as a nonlinear dual link has shown that for synchronous transport module-level-1, a signal with 8PSK modulation scheme in 6/4 GHz frequency band can provide a link margin of 3 dB or more [104]. BCH codes (255,239) deliver an increased data rate of 160.6 Mbps and reduce the carrier-to-noise ratio required for a desirable BER. But at higher bands (14.5/12.5 GHz), due to the high fade margins and greater rain susceptibility, the above link margin is not sufficient. Transmission fading due to weather impairments and multipath fading can be modelled as a Gaussian or a lognormal distribution [105].
LEO satellites encounter a mobile channel that has different Rician and Rayleigh channel characteristics than a fixed channel. These channel effects are modelled using narrowband and broadband channel models involving multi-state Markov models. The narrowband model determines three characteristics of the channel, i.e., good state, deep and intermediate shadow state [106]. An alternative 2-state model assumes a Loo distribution for the received signal and channel state characteristics based on user terminal speed [107]. Similarly, for the broadband channel, shadowing is modelled using a 2-state semi-Markov model and multipath propagation is modelled using ITU multi-tap models [108]. In general, these models assume that the channel is quasi-stationary over short periods and can be treated as a stationary stochastic process.
The congestion in existing bands makes Ka-band a viable option for SATCOM due to its anti-jamming capability, capacity, bandwidth, and smaller size requirement for the terminals [100]. Multipath and shadow fading are the main sources of signal fading in poor weather conditions. The SATCOM signal can be modelled using the Rician distribution (line-of-sight component with multipath signals and amplitude fading characteristics). There exist a number of channel models for the Ka-band SATCOM system, each with its limitations. A hybrid


10
model based on normalization of the multipath and shadowing effect is preferred. It comprises normalized Rayleigh and lognormal distributions with multiplicative Gaussian distributions for surround and weather fading. The BER performance of MPSK (BPSK, QPSK and 8PSK) has been studied for the hybrid model. Simulation results have shown that BPSK is the most error tolerant while 8PSK has the highest transmission efficiency. In a laser communication system for SATCOM, scattering particles such as dust, raindrops and fog, along with geometric transmission loss, atmospheric turbulence and pointing error are the main factors that severely degrade the link performance [109]. Atmospheric scattering can be modelled using frequency selective fading channel. In optics-based space communication, atmospheric turbulence can cause variations in the transmitted laser intensity and degrade the link. Atmospheric turbulence can be modelled using the log-normal and exponential model. The flicker in the laser can be modelled using the Gamma-Gamma distribution. Similarly, the distribution of the pointing error depends on multiple factors including the ratio of the equivalent beam radii, standard deviation of the pointing error offset, error function, beam width, beam emission angle and receiver radius.
B. GSO and NGSO SATCOM Modulation Schemes
A study of different modulation schemes including minimum-shift keying (MSK), serial MSK, BPSK, QPSK, π/4-QPSK, and offset QPSK (OQPSK) for a SATCOM modem has shown that constant envelope modulation schemes (frequency-shift keying (FSK) and phase-shift keying (PSK)) with a band-limiting filter avoid the nonlinear behaviour at high power and suppress sidelobes [42]. But FSK schemes are not suitable for SATCOM due to low bandwidth efficiency. For SATCOM systems, the constant phase modulation (CPM) is preferable to PSK due to continuous phase transition which yields fewer sidelobes. BPSK, QPSK, OQPSK, and MSK depict similar power efficiency. But parallel MSK requires phase and time synchronization and amplitude balancing. BPSK, while simple to implement, shows the worst performance in terms of bandwidth efficiency. On the other hand, MSK, with a complex implementation architecture, shows excellent immunity to noise and moderate bandwidth efficiency. π/4-QPSK and OQPSK are better suited for SATCOM than QPSK due to:
(i) smaller fluctuations in the spectrum; (ii) better immunity to nonlinear behaviour of high power amplifiers; and (iii) moderate complexity for implementation.
With new developments in the SATCOM modem in recent years, the multi-mode modem with modulation and channel coding may be used to gain power efficiency and in turn increase bandwidth efficiency. For small SDR-based SATCOM systems, adaptive coding and modulation schemes must be employed to take advantage of the time-variant SNR to achieve high throughput [99], [101], [110]–[114]. The digital video broadcast-S2 (DVB-S2) is a well known standard for SATCOM systems. The demapper algorithm for
