IV. SATCOM CHANNEL ENCODING
ITU-R has defined enhanced mobile broadband (eMBB), massive machine-type communication (mMTC) and ultrareliable low-latency communication (URLLC) as the use cases
for 5G systems [120]. Using URLLC, SATCOM systems are expected to deliver latency < 1 ms and packet loss rate < 10−5. The 3rd Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) has identified satellites as a game changer in the 5G ecosystem. Moreover, [121]–[123] have identified the non-terrestrial network (NTN) as a new feature of 5G networking systems. NTN 5G systems can:
(i) ensure service continuity for mission critical applications as well as communications on the move applications, (ii) ensure service guarantee in unserved as well as underserved areas by supplementing the terrestrial network coverage, and (iii) offer service scalability due to the wide area satellite coverage, delivering better service quality to users [124].
In the following, we provide a detailed discussion on the state-of-the-art in SATCOM channel encoding for various block lengths and the related challenges.
A. Long vs Short Block-Length Channel Encoding
To achieve the reliability and low latency requirements for 5G communication systems and satellite constellations, channel coding plays a crucial role. While a number of channel coding techniques are available, tail-biting-CC (TB-CC), lowdensity parity-check (LDPC) codes and polar codes (PC) have been recommended for 5G communication systems, in particular for URLLC and eMBB scenarios. Channel coding for long block-lengths has yielded impressive results. Modern schemes such as LDPC codes and turbo codes (TC) have shown exceptionally good performance for block lengths > 2, 000 bits with simple decoding algorithms such as the iterative belief propagation (BP) [125]–[133]. On the other hand, channel coding for short block-lengths remains an open problem due to the conflicting requirements of reliability and low latency; powerful codes with long blocklengths offer reliability at the expense of latency while short block-length codes offer low latency but compromise reliability [19], [101], [133]–[139]. Classical techniques for code construction like density evolution and extrinsic information transfer (EXIT) charts fail to deliver codes with acceptable performance due to asymptotic properties of density evolution and the failure to properly model the iterative decoder for EXIT chart analysis [135]. The most viable candidates are short binary and non-binary LDPC codes, TC, (128,64) extended BCH codes, two-TB-CC, and PC using successive cancellation list (SCL).
B. LDPC Codes for Wireless Communication
LDPC codes circumvent the need for interleavers, trellisbased decoding algorithms and cancellation lists so have lower implementation complexity as compared to TC, TB-CC and PC [19], [127], [128], [135], [136], [138], [140]–[143]. Some examples of LDPC codes include the Gallager LDPC codes, finite-geometry LDPC codes, random LDPC codes [144], algebraic LDPC codes [145], protographic LDPC codes based on EXIT (PEXIT) chart [135] and incomplete geometry based LDPC codes [146].


