LDPC codes can be constructed by decomposing large matrices (blocks) into sub-matrices which exhibit a cyclic property, i.e., rows of sub-matrices are related via cyclic shifts, leading to the quasi-cyclic LDPC (QC-LDPC) codes. Some popular examples include the finite-geometry QC-LDPC codes [144], protographic QC-LDPC codes [136], [147] and QCLDPC codes based on fundamental theorem of arithmetic [148]. Due to their structural properties, QC-LDPC codes are easier to implement as compared to other LDPC codes [105], [147]–[155].
LDPC codes based on a lower triangular matrix approximation of the parity check matrix H lead to a reduction in the encoder complexity [130]. The lower triangular form of H can be achieved rapidly using permutations of the columns or rows instead of the Gaussian elimination procedure. LDPC decoders implement a message passing algorithm between the sets of check-nodes with distinct variable nodes [131]. This not only reduces the number of iterations needed for convergence but also the memory requirement as compared to the flooding schedule and semi-serial schedule methods. A study on the BER performance of the LDPC, CC, and uncoded schemes for 16QAM and BPSK has shown that LDPC codes, with a lower complexity, outperform CC [127]. Under channel fading, [105] has discussed a multi-rate LDPC encoder where the parity check matrix H is converted to a lower triangular matrix by applying the RU algorithm. The shift-register-adder-accumulator (SRAA) reduces the encoder complexity for QC-LDPC codes as compared to TC. In the Chinese DMB-T transmission, the generator matrix G can be used in the systematic form instead of the H matrix, admitting parallel processing in the SRAA which further improves the encoding rate.
Finite geometry-based LDPC codes exhibit the cyclic property and can be decomposed into a large class of quasi-cyclic and cyclic finite geometry LDPC codes using the roots of the generator polynomial of the mother cyclic code, permutation factors and masking operations while satisfying the rowcolumn constraint on the H matrix [150]. The cyclic and quasicyclic codes have an advantage for hardware implementation as they enjoy linear complexity and can be implemented using shift registers. Construction of the H matrix from the base matrix comprising identity and zero sub-matrices has shown that in QC-LDPC codes the check node and the variable node share strictly only one edge making parallel update of likelihoods possible [142]. This has a huge advantage for high throughput and low latency requirements beyond 4G and 5G communication.
An alternative approach for constructing QC-LDPC codes is using masking with the successive cycle elimination method [156]. This method uses a dispersion-based circulant permutation matrix (CPM) where the undesired CPM in the H matrix is replaced by a zero matrix until a target masking termination condition is satisfied that achieves the desired code. Another approach for constructing QC-LDPC codes is using base graphs (BG) or protographs which are replicated to achieve larger graphs resulting in two different classes of protographic-based QC-LDPC codes, namely BG1 and BG2 [136]. A survey on decoding schedules for LDPC-CC using application specific integrated circuit (ASIC) and field programmable gate array (FPGA) implementations has shown that for optimized algorithms and in particular, the binary memoryless symmetric channel (AWGN and binary erasure) and in general for all multi-user channels, LDPC-CC is capable of achieving the decoding threshold of maximum a posteriori probability (MAP) via the BP message passing algorithm [132]. The implementation of the sum-product algorithm (SPA) for BP has high complexity due to the nonlinear tanh operations and so is not recommended for very large-scale integration (VLSI) implementation. A computationally cheaper alternative is the min-sum approximation and its variants that can yield a reduction in the hardware complexity of up to 20%. Moreover, the on-demand variable node activation (OVA) with layered based scheduling approach can overcome the inefficiency of the iterative message update schedule. To achieve a throughput of Gbps, pipelined architectures for FPGA and ASIC implementations are used but these suffer from extreme latency. Thus, are unsuitable for real time applications. In order to overcome latency, single processor LDPC-CC decoders have been studied but these are unable to achieve the desirable decoding throughput. To achieve: (i) Gbps throughput, (ii) low latency in the decoder, and (iii) low memory requirement, both schedule and node level optimization is needed. In a construction based on the PEXIT method and selective avoidance of cycles (ACE), the appropriate correction properties of protograph are selected based on the PEXIT analysis and the edges of the protographs are permuted based on the ACE method [141]. A comparison with the LDPC codes (in specification CCSDS 231.1-O-1) has shown that this approach outperforms LDPC codes with a 30% reduction in decoding complexity. Moreover, storage of only the H matrix is required, unlike LDPC codes which require storage of the G matrix. A finite geometry-based QC-LDPC code is constructed


