using an auxiliary matrix to facilitate identification and elimination of 6-cycles of the block sub-matrices obtained from EGLDPC codes [153]. Based on the randomization and circulant shift method, regular QC-LDPC codes are constructed using the XOR operation, uniform distribution of non-zero elements and pairing of rows and columns [152]. A systematic design of the protograph-based QC-LDPC codes can also be achieved by elimination of the trapping sets in a protograph [147].
C. Channel Coding Schemes for Next Generation Systems
In [135], the performance of various channel coding schemes for block length n = 128 and code dimension k = 64 has been presented in terms of coding gain and decoding complexity. This is the shortest code for satellite telecommand link standardized by CCSDS. More specifically, a comparison is shown for 128/64 binary protograph-based LDPC codes, AR3A LDPC codes, ARJA LDPC codes, (3, 6) LDPC regular codes, CCSDS LDPC codes, TC, BCH codes, TB-CC and PC. For CER = 10−4, the coding gain of CCSDS LDPC codes is low relative to that of AR3A LDPC codes, (3, 6) regular LDPC codes, and ARJA LDPC codes. However, for the satellite telecommand link, error detection is more critical than coding gain and so CCSDS LDPC codes, which achieve low error rate, CER = 10−6 compared to other LDPC codes, are preferred. Also, for CER = 10−4, TC outperform binary LDPC codes but are outperformed by BCH codes. PC with cyclic redundancy check (CRC) outperform all other codes for CER = 10−4 and CER = 10−6. TB-CC codes with order statistic decoding (OSD) achieve the performance of BCH and LDPC codes. With respect to decoding complexity, the performance of iterative decoding is satisfactory for binary LDPC codes and TC with the advantage of hardware implementation. For non-binary LDPC codes, the FFT-based decoders are ≈ 64× more complex than the decoders for CCSDS LDPC codes. However, the complexity of the FFT-based decoder can be reduced using sub-optimal check node update rules. OSD and non-binary BP decoding algorithms have similar complexity for short block-lengths but OSD may be impractical for long block-lengths. For URLLC, mMTC, and eMBB scenarios in 5G with block size in the range [40, 1000) bits, a comparison of 3GPP LTE standard TC, LDPC codes, PC, and CC based on the BER performance for a given code rate and modulation technique has shown that selection of the coding scheme depends on the block length [138]. For short block-length (40 bits) using QPSK and 16 QAM with code rate 1/3 and BLER = 10−6, PC and LDPC codes exhibit the best performance. But for the lower code rate of 1/12, PC with SCL decoding outperform all other schemes. PC with SCL depict 0.25-2 dB improved margin with respect to other coding schemes. We note that for LDPC codes, the CRC is not required since the syndrome calculation is sufficient to validate the decoded codeword. However, the performance of LDPC codes is limited by the shortest girth, as in the case of progressive edge-growth (PEG). On the other hand, for medium-block length (200 bits) and code rates 1/3, 1/12, TC outperform LDPC, PC and CC. LDPC shows the second best performance with 0.5 dB loss.
With respect to decoding complexity, LDPC codes depict better performance with the min-sum algorithm than with SPA. The soft input Viterbi algorithm for code rate R = 1/3, length = 9 and generator polynomials 557oct, 663oct, and 711oct gives better performance for terminated CC. The inclusion of SCL results in a noticeable improvement in the performance of PC. Under reliability and high throughput requirements, CC with bitwise map decoder based on the Bahl-Cocke-Jelinek-Raviv (BCJR) algorithm, PC with successive cancellation, and QCLDPC codes with SPA for decoding, have shown similar BER performance for BPSK in AWGN channel [143]. We note that unlike CC, the decoder implementation for TC also requires an interleaver. The decoder uses the quadratic permutation polynomials (QPP) with two soft input and soft output (SISO) decoders. For QC-LDPC codes and repeat-accumulate codes, SPA gives satisfactory results. In 3G/4G communication, the primary coding scheme is TC, obtained from a permutation of CC with trellis-based one-dimensional graphical structure decoding. However, for high throughput requirements of 5G communication, TC are replaced by LDPC codes which support multiple block sizes and rates while observing strict performance parameters with reasonable complexity. LDPC codes have nice structural properties that are amenable to parallelization in hardware as compared to TC. Moreover, LDPC codes are conveniently defined using the parity check matrix H and Tanner graphs. The construction of QC-LDPC codes can be achieved by replicating the BG with permutations. The latest LDPC codes for 5G communication are essentially a concatenation of the LDPC codes with a low-density generator matrix (LDGM). During the check node update procedure the updated variable node may not be available so it is not efficient for decoding. However, in quasi-row orthogonality construction, the orthogonality between consecutive layers avoids dependency and so is efficient for decoding. Consid