V. SOFTWARE-DEFINED RADIO BASED SATCOM
SYSTEMS
A software-defined radio (SDR) is a hardware device which implements signal processing methods such as digital conversion, upsampling, modulation, filtering, and detection using software-based algorithms. An SDR has the capability to perform partial or full physical layer function of a radio using modifiable software and programmable hardware [16], [158]. Once programmed, unlike a normal radio, an SDR has the
flexibility to reprogram its working architecture to evolve with advanced communication protocols, channel assignment policies and wireless functionalities [16]. The signal processing algorithms can be implemented using tools such as WARP, NI LabVIEW, NI USRP, GNU Radio, IMEC SDR, Lyrtech SDR Platform, etc. Recent developments focus on reconfigurable SDR System-on-Chip ground receivers with the ultimate aim of Satellite-on-Chip [159], [160]. We begin with a brief review of SDR for wireless transmission and then turn to SDR-based SATCOM systems. In [161], the authors have analysed the JPEG file transfer over a wireless channel using the GNU Radio, a software development platform for Universal Software Radio Peripheral (USRP) devices. The transfer protocols are implemented using the GNU Radio libraries. The differential binary-PSK scheme with a bit rate of 500 kbps and packet size of 1500 bytes was used for transmitting the file. The received file was found to be distorted due to packet loss during the transmission so this approach was neither robust against channel interference and duplication nor robust for packet recovery. The USRPbased wireless transmission protocol implementing buffering and leaky bucket algorithm showed significant improvement in transmission error and environmental interference problems [162]. The setup includes three nodes with a transmitter, receiver, and repeater. A Python/C++ environment is used for the development platform. The simplified wrapper and interface generator is used to interface Python and C++. The presence of a buffer helps to receive the correct transferred file size. Furthermore, an identification for the sending and receiving modules was added for multi-node communication in addition to time delay between transmission of successive packets. The buffering and leaky bucket algorithm control the flow and time for sending several packets. Thus, mitigating distortion and duplication issues arising in transmission over a wireless channel. SDR prototyping platforms over heterogeneous and multicore processors overcome the challenges associated with central processing unit (CPU)-related computational complexity [163]. The 20 megasamples per second (Msps) I-Q data are upsampled to 400 Msps and transmitted at RF band. At the SDR receiver, signals are converted to baseband I-Q components using a direct conversion receiver (DCR). The digitization of a signal is performed at 100 Msps using a 14-bit analog-to-digital converter (ADC) with real-time processing handled using an FPGA. The existing literature on SDR for SATCOM has discussed the challenges as well as advantages of small satellites [99], [102], [110]–[112], [114], [126], [159], [162], [164]–[170]. Real-time implementation issues in terms of computational capability and processing delay of an SDR have also been studied [159], [162], [163], [169]–[172]. These studies have concluded that multicore and heterogeneous processors can overcome real-time challenges. But an SDR implementation of the multiple access control (MAC)/physical layer of wireless/SATCOM networking has not received much attention. The virtualization of the MAC/physical layer can be implemented using an SDR for space related applications [173]. In the following, we review


