Channel coding is an important component of SATCOM systems, therefore it has received considerable attention for SDR-based applications [174], [175]. DVB-S2 is one of the most popular protocols for SATCOM systems and has been investigated for transceiver design in [176], [177]. One of the popular error correcting codes used in DVB-S2 and DVB-S2X standards are the LDPC codes which have shown BER performance close to the Shannon limit with low complexity and low latency. The challenges relating to practical implementation of LDPC codes have been overcome by the flexibility of the SDR approach. Simulation of small satellite constellation via an SDR is an emerging research area. The reconfiguration capability of SDR is useful for waveform design and to ascertain synchronization parameters [178].
A. SDR for the GSO SATCOM System
Only a few studies have been conducted on SDR-based testbeds for the analysis of SATCOM systems for both communication on the pause and communication on the move applications [99], [102], [114], [126], [166], [168]–[170]. Most of the work has focused on NGSO, i.e., small LEO satellites [38]–[40], [99], [101], [113], [114], [126], [133], [137]–[139],


16
[166], [168], [169]. Popular FPGA platforms used in these SDRs are the Zync 7000 hybrid ARM/FPGA and KINTEX7410T FPGA [30], [117]. In [172], an SDR-based system for the GEO-orbit satellite has been developed. The payload signal is the DVB-S signal using the QPSK modulation scheme and the ranging signal is BPSK modulated with PN sequence with a symbol rate of 2.048 Msps and 70 MHz bandwidth. The study has shown that the use of PN techniques for ranging measurements allows the same bandwidth for both ranging and payload signal with negligible impairment to the latter. For the geostationary orbit, DVB-S and ranging signal can be treated as additive noise with respect to each other. Moreover, for a measurement time of 1 s and root mean square error of about 25 cm, the relative power of the payload to the ranging signals is measured to be as small as −18 dB. In [159], an SDR-based distributed satellite system has been studied where the link is evaluated using BPSK with concatenated codes comprising Viterbi (1/2) and Reed Solomon (RS) codes (160,128) for symbol rates of 1, 2.4, 4.8, 9.6, and 19.2k. A performance comparison using FPGA and CPU for the distributed satellite system has shown that an embedded system architecture is suitable for the earth stations favouring transition in communication from a single satellite to multisatellites. Distributed and mobile ground terminals can be deployed to enhance the access time. Moreover, an embedded design for future SDR-based distributed SATCOM systems is required to compensate for the development requirements. In [179], SDR emulators for channel modelling of GEO satellites have been developed using USRP and LabVIEW IDE. The emulator models the channel using the thermal noise characteristics, phase noise simulating rotation of symbols, propagation delay encountered during transmission and furthermore, processes a 1.6 MBaud signal stream. Interference mitigation for GEO satellite systems has been studied based on acquisition, measurement and mitigation of RF interference. The infrastructure has been developed using several devices and a hybrid approach; the payload emulator is based on BEECube’s BEE4, the channel emulator is based on the singlelink satellite channel emulator RT Logic T400CS, and the transceiver is based on the platforms provided by Nutaq Inc. Interference mitigation schemes can be found in [180]–[182]. In an SDR based wide-band radio multi-system avionics architecture for the satellite link, [181] has used adaptive coding and modulation to establish the communication link. In order to reduce the interference generated from multiple sources in the wireless communication system, the so-called multilinear subspace estimation and projection algorithm has been proposed that outperforms projection based radio frequency interference detection algorithms at the cost of a slightly higher complexity [180], [182]. Multi-beam capability is important for high throughput systems. The SERENADE NI USRP based on an end-to-end multi-beam SATCOM emulator has been used to evaluate precoding and interference mitigation strategies [183]–[185]. The transponder for LEO to GEO orbit is emulated for forward link via DVB-S2X emulators with multi-beam capability and multiple user terminals. In [186], an SDR-based receiver frontend architecture has
been designed for broadband SATCOM systems. A multi-core SDR architecture offers flexibility and low production cost for GNSS receivers [187]. Besides SDR-based modules for source coding, modulation, channel coding and multiplexing, SoftSpace based MAC/physical-layer function virtualization has been designed and developed on FPGA and signal processing boards in space forwarding devices [188]. The interoperability of these devices can be promoted by abstracting the MAC/physical layer functions via reprogrammable and independent SDR technology having signal processing and resource sharing capabilities [173]. The use of SDR in these devices can enhance the adaptive features of the underlying communication technology, reconfiguration of the physical layer parameters and optimization of resource utilization. Similarly, in heterogeneous network topology for SATCOM systems, SDR can provide the capability of far-end upgrades, reconfiguration of implemented radios and multi-mode operations. In [189], an SDR-based prototype design of a SATCOM modem for geostationary satellites with a bent-pipe satellite transponder and LABVIEW-based base-band signal generation and processing architecture has been discussed. The proposed design accommodates multiple provisions such as variable length message signal, variable packet structure, option of CRC, RS codes for short block-lengths at various rates, DSSS with various modulation schemes including BPSK, QPSK and 8PSK at various transmission rates and various reliability requirements. In addition, the proposed design incorporates anti-jamming capability and user anonymity along with selfreliance. The path loss has been modelled using AWGN and a delay of 250 ms has been incorporated to model the propagation delay in the SATCOM channel. USRP-X310 is used for the hardware implementation. The prototype is a testing platform for communication in both static and dynamic GEO, MEO and LEO environments.
B. SDR for the Non-GSO SATCOM System
The feasibility of a low cost SDR prototype for the ultrahigh frequency (UHF) and very high frequency (VHF) bands using commercially available off-the-shelf (COTS) components has been investigated in [110]. Doppler shift was identified as one of the main challenges in the communication between the ground station and LEO satellites. Communication with the LEO space segment requires software controlled (Ham Radio Deluxe) tracking antennas that can be steered in realtime to the predicted satellite location and can automatically adjust to compensate for the Doppler shift. A low cost solution can have some performance limitations in terms of signal processing, multi-frequency communication and multi-mission capabilities. In [111], an SDR-based ground station transceiver (USRP N210) has been studied which incorporates the design of the mission control center for the European Student Earth Orbiter (ESEO) project by developing an SDR-based earth station segment. The design is based on amateur radio frequencies and AX.25 communication protocol and can be used for downlink and uplink transmissions. All digital functions are


