performed on an FPGA board with modulation schemes that are UHF-band compliant pulse coded modulation with 0.5 bandwidth-time factor for Gaussian frequency-shift keying (GFSK) having non-return to zero characteristics. The ground station can support inter-satellite communication at UHF-band in the LEO. Simulations of communication between multiple satellites orbiting in LEO using DS-CDMA, Rayleigh fading, and AWGN channels using QPSK and BPSK with CC have shown that an adaptive and reconfigurable communication system for the Inter Satellite Link (ISL) can achieve high data rate [99]. The design parameters include orbital velocity ∼ 3 km/s, with distance between neighbouring satellites < 3 km, transmission range 10-20 km, data rate 3-8 Mbps, and transmission delay 150-300 ms. The challenge for small satellites is the limited available power. For transmission power 0.5-3 W and S-band, it is 1-2.5 W for 1U CubeSat and maximum 2-5 W for 2U CubeSat. The SATCOM channel is simulated using AWGN and Rayleigh fading models. In order to access the channel for inter-satellite communication, collision free schemes such as FDMA, TDMA, and CDMA, and contention based protocols such as ALOHA, carrier sense multiple access (CSMA), busy tone multiple access (BTMA), and idle signal multiple access (ISMA) may be used. However, contention based protocols are not suitable for real-time or near real-time applications. DS-CDMA due to its multiple user access with independent, uncorrelated pseudorandom codes can overcome the collision issue in multi-satellite communication. This is in contrast to FDMA and TDMA which require separate bandwidth and strict timing synchronization for multiple data rates. A software-defined transmitter for a cube satellite based on high-end FPGA enhances the signal processing and flexible structure capability [167]. A performance comparison of BFSK and GMSK has shown that BFSK has the advantage of demodulation, low bandwidth, and higher efficiency at 9.6k baud rate. On the other hand, GMSK depicts good spectral efficiency due to the constant envelope feature and shows resilience against the amplitude base noise with moderate receiver complexity. Moreover, GMSK can be used with nonlinear amplifiers providing low-cost solution and highly efficient C-type amplifiers and is suitable for both non-coherent and coherent detection systems. A model of a nano-SATCOM system based on the GNU Radio, HACK RF, and Raspberry Pi platform with GMSK, is a low-cost solution that is limited by amateur UHF RF and the size of the communication device [169]. The evolution in payload, telecommunication architecture, communication, and network protocols, all indicate to an increased use of small satellites for telecommunication services [126]. The utilization of higher frequency bands, optical communication, and advancements in protocols and architectures are some emerging trends in the small satellite field. Considering payload evolution, we find a focus on frequency band and antenna evolution, laser communication terminals for small satellites, and SDR payloads. The use of W-band (75110 GHz) using horn antennas for small satellites is a modern trend. Similarly, the use of free space optical communication which can provide higher data rates at lower weight and power
consumption (10 Gbps within an order of 5 kg weight and 50 W power). For small LEO SATCOM systems, SDR-based adaptive communication using 16-amplitude phase shift keying (APSK), 8-PSK, QPSK, and BPSK schemes with coding rates 7/8, 5/4, 3/4, 2/3, and 1/2 using CC has shown that during an orbit the distance between the space and earth terminals varies resulting in changes in the SNR [113]. For maximum channel utilization, an adaptive modulation scheme can be used with high modulation like 16APSK when maximum SNR is available and low modulation BPSK when the SNR is critical with a feedback channel to change the transmission mode. However, incorrect frames observed may be due to the presence of noise, synchronization loss and rapidly changing Doppler shift. These issues can be eliminated employing additional channel coding and decoding, improvements in the synchronization algorithm at the receiver side, and implementation of good carrier tracking algorithms along with the use of short frames. Note that the use of these methods can enhance the transmission rate by about 2×. A survey on communication and modulation protocols for small satellites has shown that due to the growing demand for small satellite missions, the traditional point-to-point communication protocols such as AX.25 reach their limit and novel, more efficient protocols are needed [114]. A lowcost SDR solution can accommodate the adaptive nature of communication in terms of modulation parameters and error coding capabilities with respect to the given channel conditions. Moreover, the NGHam packet architecture with RS error-correcting codes may be used to overcome the bottleneck. In the communication link for a LEO satellite, a carrier synchronization algorithm using non-linear operations can recover the LEO carrier [102]. Addressing the limitations of short visibility of LEO space segments, multiple ground stations at geographically diverse locations can enhance the availability time. However, this requires considerable ground infrastructure. An alternative approach based on adaptive modulation (MPSK/M-APSK) can be used to enhance the communication data rate. However, the Doppler shift is the main challenge for adaptive modulation schemes. A 1 kHzps change of Doppler shift demands a synchronization algorithm; otherwise, the advantages of high SNR cannot be achieved. A second-order type-2 phase lock loop (PLL) can track the carrier. In addition, a frequency correction block is required to remove the frequency shifts using FPGA devices without the assistance of a frequency locked loop. Spectrum reuse and dynamic spectrum access are important considerations for next generation networks to achieve the goal of high-speed SATCOM [28]. The available satellite spectrum can be reused in a much more efficient way by deploying multi-beam, multi-band heterogeneous satellite transmissions and multi-orbit satellite systems. SDRs can be deployed for multi-beam satellite communication systems where interference between beam-cells can be mitigated via software control. Similarly, the heterogeneous nature of multi-band transmissions can be achieved using software switching of operating frequencies. For multi-orbit satellite systems, SDRs can be used to obtain GEO and non-GEO operational capabilities at


