the software level. In [190], an SDR-based LEO satellite receiver has been designed that can process data non-causally, implement MF for demodulation, and apply course Doppler compensation for full Doppler search. The MF enables the proposed SDR design to adapt to demodulate multiple modulation schemes such as FSK, MSK, GMSK and BPSK. The MF can be designed by curve fitting the received signal. The computational cost for the Doppler search is high so the NVIDIA Quadro K620 GPUs are used to reduce the processing time. In order to extract the Doppler measurements for LEO multi-constellation systems, an adaptive SDR-based receiver has been designed and developed in [191]. The signals in LEO satellites have a higher SNR as compared to GEO and MEO satellite signals which is useful for positioning systems. However, communication is limited by satellite visibility and signal coverage. A multi-constellation positioning system can overcome these limitations. The authors have proposed the following procedure: (i) detect available satellites by using Welch’s power spectral density, (ii) PLL-based tracking of Doppler frequency for available satellites, and (iii) select positioning measurements using switching filters. Extended Kalman filtering (EKF) is used in receivers for tracking Iridium NEXT and ORBCOMM satellites. The ORBCOMM satellites operate in the VHF band with 137-138 MHz bandwidth with QPSK modulation and 4.8 kbps downlink data rate in a continuous mode of transmission. The Iridium NEXT constellation operates in 1616-1626.5 MHz with QPSK in burst mode. The adaptive design for multi-constellation receiver exploits the different modes of transmission, burst based simplex signal for Iridium-NEXT and continuous signal based transmission for ORBCOMM satellites [192], [193]. In order to support these multi-constellation, a switching mode for the EKF is designed which determines the available satellite based on the type and dimension of the measurement matrix. To test the performance of the receiver, bladeRF is used with low-noise amplifiers for Iridium NEXT satellite channel and RF bandpass filter for the ORBCOMM satellites. Considering the static mode, for ring alert burst mode the positioning error is reduced from 765 m to 213 m and for messaging burst modes it is reduced to 132 m resulting in a 72% improvement. In [117], an SDR-based baseband system for satellite telemetry receiver and telecommand transmitter has been designed using the GNU Radio platform. The system has been validated using low cost SDR platform (USRP X310), and mission qualified satellite emulator in a lab environment and infield test were performed on orbiting satellites. The telecommand transmitter consist of three main units: (i) mission console centre (MCC) data transfer interface, (ii) FEC and frame generation block, and (iii) bit modulation block. Similarly, the telemetry receiver consist of three main blocks: (i) a bit synchronization block, (ii) FEC and frame synchronization, and (iii) MCC data delivery interface. The hardware test bed is based on the USRP X310 which is interfaced with the GNU Radio running on a Linux platform
