ps, 0.57 s for 4.8 kbps and 1.13 s for 2.4 kbps. This resulted in a patch size of 50 for the proposed design in a single transmission. The timeout for reading the packets for the desirable packet success rate was observed to be 300 ms.
VI. FUTURE DIRECTIONS AND OPEN PROBLEMS
SATCOM systems can play an important role in achieving the objective of advanced wireless communication systems (i.e., 5G, 6G and beyond). These systems are being extensively explored in integrated-satellite terrestrial networks for 5G and 6G networks [196]. The main challenges are synchronization issues, resource allocation, channel modelling, trajectory optimization, interference issues, and new communication protocols for integrated systems [23], [197], [198]. Machine learning techniques for satellite applications, i.e., satellite operations, weather monitoring, earth observations and sensor fusion for navigation have the potential to enhance signal processing and resource allocation and furthermore, address the complexity issues in satellite networks [30]. In this section, we discuss future directions with respect to channel access methodologies, modulation schemes, channel encoding schemes, and SDR based applications and identify open problems.


19
A. Channel Access Methodologies
Advanced wireless networks aim to support high throughput (20-100 Gbps), better spectral efficiency (30 bits/Hz for downlink and 15 bits/Hz for uplink), optimum power consumption, hybrid systems (satellite and terrestrial systems), low latency (≤ 1 ms), communication reliability and high mobility (500-1000 km/h) [18], [24], [196]. Advanced channel access methodologies such as OFDMA, NOMA, WDMA, SDMA and rate splitting multiple access (RSMA) can play a pivotal role in achieving the above aims by enhancing flexibility and efficient use of the available spectrum. OFDMA is a potential multiplexing scheme for future wireless communication systems [24]. It encounters multipath fading issues resulting in ISI for wireless and satellite communication. The addition of a CP can reduce ISI at the cost of transmission overhead. In addition, there is a power overhead, reducing the power efficiency of the system. SFBC-OFDM and NCC-OFDMA are variants of OFDMA that can resolve the multipath fading issue [60], [61]. OFDM also suffers from effective side lobe levels resulting in out-of-band (OOB) radiations (that are high amplitudes outside the required spectrum) reducing the spectral efficiency [199]. Multiple variants of OFDM such as F-OFDMA, GFDM, and TIBWB-OFDM have been considered for 5G and beyond wireless communication network [24], [67], [200]–[203]. SP-OFDM can provide secure transmission in the presence of a jammer [78]. F-OFDMA can potentially reduce the OOB radiations using spectrum shaping filters at the cost of complexity and interferences [62]–[64]. GFDM can enhance spectral efficiency due to requirement of only one CP at the cost of ISI, ICI, PAPR, and implementation complexity [18], [67]. To address these issues and to make it suitable for future systems, multiple equalization and interference cancellation techniques with advanced architecture have been proposed [66], [67], [67]–[73]. TIBWB-OFDM is an attractive choice for enhancing higher spectral and power efficiency [74]–[76]. However, further research is required to investigate suitability for future wirelesses communication systems [77]. With the advancements in satellite constellation systems and integration of SATCOM architecture in 5G terrestrial scenarios, NOMA and its variants, e.g., power domain, code domain (sparse code multiple access (SCMA), low density spreading (LDS), multi-user shared access (MUSA)), pattern division, lattice partition and bit division [196], can be extended to SATCOM systems for attaining high throughput with the available bandwidth. The application of NOMA in layered division multiplexing, a new multiplexing scheme proposed by the advanced television systems committee (ATSC) for terrestrial digital TV broadcasting systems, is a good example [204]. Similarly, for scheduling of user terminals, suboptimal algorithms have been proposed to maximize the overloaded SATCOM system capacity [205]. In [206], the overlay coding technique is used to mitigate interference in multi-beam SATCOM systems based on NOMA channel access techniques. In [207], the channel conditions for different users have been studied for relaying data in a satellite-terrestrial relay system. A user with a high channel gain can relay data to another
user with poor channel condition resulting in low outage probability and greater ergodic capacity compared to OMA. In recent advancements, integration of NOMA and coordinated multi-point (CoMP) transmissions has been studied to measure the performance of NOMA in terms of ergodic rates, outage probabilities, maximizing sum-rates, and dynamic power allocation strategies [208]–[210]. In this regard two popular schemes have been analyzed, the Alamouti-NOMA and the joint-transmission NOMA [211]. Recent advancements in satellite-aerial-terrestrial networks accommodate unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) enabled networks to enhance system throughput, maximize the terrestrial user’s achievable rate, to relay the desired information, and interference cancellation [22], [212]–[214]. However, extension of NOMA for terrestrial 5G networks to SATCOM systems and constellations may require modification to the channel propagation models due to similar path loss for multiple ground stations [3], user pairing strategies based on channel gains [82], complexity of design with respect to multi-beam capability, design constraints (onboard power resources, propagation delays, signal distortion and mobility issues), optimal beam forming and capability in terms of allocation of onboard resources [215]. The demand for higher capacity, low latency, ultra-high reliability and massive connectivity for next generation wireless communication networks requires next generation multiple access schemes [216]. In the unsourced multiple access scheme [217], a sparse Tanner graph based transmission scheme has been proposed for MIMO channels. The proposed scheme has a lower complexity and outperforms the compressed sensing based unsourced multiple access scheme. The device selection and resource allocation for non-uniform device based on federated meta-learning algorithm outperforms baseline algorithms [218]. Considering the MAC, the QMIX-advanced listen-before-talk (QLBT) protocol achieves improved throughput, smaller delay and low jitter [219]. The proposed protocol uses multi-agent reinforcement learning for centralized training and decentralized execution. Rainbow link, a novel FDMA-based access scheme, maps resources to specific spatial directions, creating subsets of OFDMA subcarriers and appreciate beamforming gain for the complete array [220]. The proposed scheme does not require explicit beam training, and supports random access. Thus, is promising for latency critical applications. The RSMA technique for visible light communication compensates for the limited modulation bandwidth for single and multi-cell networks and has proven to be energy efficient compared to benchmark schemes [221]. To summarize, next-generation multiple access (NGMA) requires extensive research efforts in the field of emerging multiplexing schemes such as NOMA and SDMA, reconfigurable and advanced modulation and channel coding schemes, and application of machine learning tools for NGMA requirements. Optical technologies offer an alternative approach to overcome RF band bottleneck in SATCOM by providing 10 THz bandwidth and have the potential to be the enabling technology for future digitization revolution. However, atmospheric attenuation in the RF band at higher frequencies is the main challenge which can be mitigated using the site di


