versity approach. The prospect of optical technologies in high throughput satellite systems can deliver high data rates (Tbps throughput) for GEO communication systems using dense wave division multiplexing (DWDM) [222]. For homodyne receivers and using QPSK modulation, 80 Gbps throughput has been achieved. The emerging needs of global coverage and throughput cannot be met using existing GEO satellite systems due to the limited RF band spectrum, i.e., 1 GHz bandwidth in the Ku-band (12-18 GHz) and 6 GHz in the Kaband (27-40 GHz) which is shared by government, military and terrestrial fixed services. Higher bands, e.g., Q/V (4050 GHz) allocate 8 GHz bandwidth for broadband services without exclusive allocation and also share the spectrum with military and terrestrial services [223]. SATCOM is based on the pointing mechanism. Different point-ahead angles (PAA) exist for GEO (18 μrad), MEO (2030 μrad) and LEO (30-60 μrad) satellites. A large PAA limits the pointing performance and imposes higher transmission power to overcome the divergence of the uplink beam. A link budget performance for GEO, LEO and MEO satellites depicts that 2.3 dB less transmitted power is required for LEO satellites compared to GEO satellites. For MEO satellites, the difference is 6–14 dB depending on the pointing mechanism, i.e., DL-tracking pointing and open-loop pointing. The main challenge for optical communication is atmospheric turbulence. To demonstrate the terabit scale between the GEO satellite and the optical ground station, a 2 degree elevation was considered in [224] to emulate the optical SATCOM link under the worst turbulence conditions. Forty DWDM channel with 100 GHz capacity each, modulated with OnOff keying (OOK) at modulation rate of 43 Gbps achieved a throughput of 1.72 Tbps. A pre-amplification of the coherent reception increased throughput to 13.16 Tbps using the DP16 QAM modulation scheme for 53 channels modulated at 34 GBaud. Moreover, digital homodyne processing used for an extra channel of 100 GHz with 80 Gbps and QPSK modulation scheme. Multiple modulation schemes i.e., DPQPSK, DP-8QAM and DP-16QAM were tested to determine successful use of higher order modulation schemes under strong atmospheric turbulence conditions. In integrated SATCOM systems, and 5G and 6G networks, terrestrial networks are an attractive option to resolve satellite spectrum scarcity and provide high speed, seamless global services. The integration of satellites with non-terrestrial platforms (e.g., UAVs), high altitude platforms, and terrestrial platforms can enhance the wireless coverage, provide high-speed data rates with broadband connectivity, and act as a backup route for sustainable communication [25], [29], [31], [197], [225], [226]. However, efficient network access schemes are required that can overcome interference and congestion issues. In this regard, satellite spectrum sensing capability, grouping of users into primary and secondary, satellite beamforming technology, and satellite receiver design are some areas that need to be explored further.
B. Modulation Schemes
Digital modulation schemes with constant envelope (MPSK and M-FSK) are suitable for SATCOM systems since
these are not severely distorted in the non-linear region of the high power amplifiers. For M = 2k with k ∈ {1, 2, ..., 6}, MPSK has shown good spectral efficiency. But with a higher modulation index, the signals are more sensitive to channel impairments due to the proximity of the constellation points. Moreover, the system complexity is also increased. The most popular schemes used for SATCOM are QPSK and its variant π/4-QPSK and OQPSK due to their better spectral efficiency and resilience against channel noise and interference. FSK schemes are not suitable for SATCOM systems due to the low bandwidth efficiency. MSK and GFSK are two popular FSK schemes used in SATCOM systems where bandwidth efficiency is traded-off for lower system complexity. A higher M depicts better data transmission capacity, but for the allocated bandwidth, the receiver shows high sensitivity due to the reduced frequency deviation. It is for this reason that a continuous phase modulation scheme is preferred to avoid abrupt transitions in the phase which result in the lower sidelobe energy. For non-constant envelopes, e.g., M-QAM, the information is decoded by varying both the amplitude and phase. As compared to 16PSK, 16-QAM depicts higher resilience to channel impairments due to the greater separation between the constellation points. However, non-constant envelope requires wider linear operational range for high power amplifier (HPA) resulting in increased cost and complexity. M-APSK for M = 16, 32, 64 is also used in modern SATCOM systems. APSK can overcome signal impairment issues suffered by PSK and requires a smaller linear operational range for HPA compared to QAM due to fewer amplitude levels. For example, 16-APSK has only 2 amplitude levels while QAM has 3. Similarly, 32-APSK has 3 amplitude levels compared to 5 levels of QAM. However, these schemes require high-end algorithms for packet detection and synchronization. Orthogonal timefrequency space (OTFS), a recent two-dimensional modulation scheme, can provide high speed, high frequency and large antenna array transmissions [227]. OTFS can overcome the time-varying issues of the channel and can provide nearconstant channel gain due to time-independent characteristics [196]. Adaptive coding and modulation (ACM) for enhancing transmission efficiency and error tolerance is a modern technique. It improves the system power efficiency, reduces interuser interferences and can meet variable data rate requirements [27], [228]. ACM can provide higher data rates, particularly for small satellites which operate in the NGSO where channel SNR is time-varying. Selective retransmission under ACM coupled with OFDM signals can enhance the spectral efficiency by partial retransmission of failed packets [228]. ISL will play a pivotal role for advanced SATCOM systems. Enhanced spectral efficiency, power efficiency and improved BER can be achieved using ACM for these links. ACM can achieve 6× the throughput of constant modulation and coding scheme [229]. To overcome the distortions in ACM due to QAM, higher-order partial response continuous phase modulation can be used to enhance power and spectral efficiency [230]. The higher-frequency bands with ACM may be used [126]. The authors have stated that apart from a few


