theoretical studies, there have been no in-depth analyses of ACM in terms of optimization of the adaptive modulation and coding technology. Moreover, the scarcity problem for RF band and synchronization issue may be reduced by using visible light communication, having higher data rates with laser communication.
C. Channel Encoding Schemes
Reliable and low latency communication are crucial for future communication systems involving satellite constellations, 5G, and 6G, which highlights the importance of channel coding. While TB-CC and TC have been popular choices for LTE, LDPC codes, PC, TB-CC, and TC are recommended for 5G URLLC and eMBB systems. Recently, rateless codes have emerged as a potential contender for future wireless communication systems [32]. LDPC codes and TC have shown exceptionally good performance for block lengths > 2, 000 bits [125]–[133]. But these codes lead to high latency. The design for short block-lengths is an open problem due to conflicting requirements of reliability and low latency [19], [101], [133]–[139]. Latency in channel coding can be reduced by optimizing the contributing factors such as time-transmit latency, processing latency and retransmission time [137] that depend on throughput and error performance of the channel decoder. QC-LDPC codes are a popular choice for the analysis of the short block-length regime [152] which outperform TC with a lower complexity [231], [232]. A recent variant of QCLDPC codes, the so-called protograph-based raptor-like LDPC (PBRL-LDPC) codes is a promising choice for low latency applications due to fewer decoding iterations required [233]. The stringent requirements of URLLC and mMTC can be met by PC which have depicted reliability of 99.99% with very low power requirements compared to LDPC, TC and TB-CC codes [234]. Parity-check PC is the strongest candidate from variants of PC including extended BCH (eBCH) and parity-checkconcatenated PC for all block lengths and code rates [235].The concatenation of PC, LDPC, and spinal codes (variant of rateless codes), and hybridization of PC and turbo product codes have depicted promising results for low latency, reduced implementation complexity, and enhanced power efficiency [236]–[238]. CRC-aided spinal codes with TB structures reduce decoding complexity compared to conventional spinal codes and therefore need further exploration [239]. Extensive research on short-block length codes has revealed that Polyansky-Poor-Verdu (PPV) limits have not been achieved by any channel codes [240], [241]. Hybridization of PC and LDPC codes needs to be explored further to meet the requirements of URLLC and mMTC services [235]. The complex implementation of TC limits their application for future wireless communication systems. However, if their complexity can be reduced, they may prove useful for high throughput wireless communication systems [242], [243].
D. SDR Implementation of SATCOM Systems
Future wireless communication systems, i.e., integrated satellite systems, 5G and 6G require reconfigurability, adaptivity and flexibility. For advanced GSO satellite systems, SDRs
can be useful for optimization of the available bandwidth and modulation schemes, multi-satellite transmissions, high throughput using multi-beam transmissions, and interference mitigation capabilities [172], [182]–[185]. In addition to interference mitigation, SDR testbeds can also be used for airplane connectivity [180], [181]. Pre-coding techniques for power minimization and multi-user multi-communication system can be analysed using SDR testbeds for advanced SATCOM systems [244], [245].
The evolution in payload, telecommunication architecture, communication, and network protocols, all indicate to an increased use of small satellites for telecommunication services [126]. In multi-constellation systems, delay-tolerant networking (DTN) protocols can use ring road architecture. However, non-DTN and DTN architectures demand proper integration for successful communication between multiple space and ground segments. The softwarisation of the network can be achieved via network function virtualization (NFV) and software defined network (SDN) architectures. Moreover, NFV/SDN protocols may play a key role in 5G communication, if fully investigated.
Advances in satellite constellation systems have increased the coverage of small satellite systems. SDRs provide the platform to explore aspects of practical implementation [102]. FPGA compatible SDR payloads provide reconfigurable platforms for emerging CubSAT platforms (GomSpace, NanoAvionics, AlenSpace, ISISpace). LEO satellites offer smaller propagation delay, lower atmospheric attenuation, coverage area limitations, free space loss and lower launching cost. SDRs can:
• be used to explore the viability of higher frequency bands, laser communication, and novel communication protocols and architecture for future systems [30], [114], [126], [194]; • enhance throughput and spectral efficiency by using multi-beam and multi-band heterogeneous satellite transmissions for multi-orbit satellite systems [28]; • be used for analysing the Doppler shift in inter-satellite communication systems and satellite-terrestrial advanced networking systems; • assist in modelling the multi-constellation receivers capable of supporting multiple modulation schemes [190]; • be used to explore modern channel encoding techniques, waveform designs for fulfilling the stringent requirements of URLLC, mMTC and eMBB; • be used to explore practical implementation of LDPC codes for advanced satellite systems [174], [175]; • provide the capability of pulse shaping optimization and multi-component signalling for designing modern waveforms [178]; • be used to study the interference mitigation capabilities and techniques for SATCOM systems [179]; • be utilized with heterogeneous processors and multi-core processors to evaluate interference mitigation techniques and to enhance the computational performance [162], [163], [171], [172].


